Octopuses and squid rewrite their own RNA at a rate seen in no other animals, altering the proteins their nerve cells make without changing the underlying genes. One research team argues that this is an elegant solution to life in variable seas: an octopus can retune its nervous system to the cold within hours rather than waiting generations for a useful mutation.

Text 2
A second team agrees that RNA editing lets cephalopods adjust quickly but points to a hidden price. For editing to work, the genetic sequence around each editing site must stay unchanged, so those regions accumulate far fewer mutations over evolutionary time. What makes an octopus flexible today may leave its lineage less able to change tomorrow.', 'Based on the texts, how would the second team most likely respond to the first team''s description of RNA editing as ''an elegant solution''?', '[{"key":"A","text":"By denying that RNA editing allows octopuses to adjust to changes in temperature"},{"key":"B","text":"By arguing that the solution comes at the cost of reduced long-term evolutionary flexibility"},{"key":"C","text":"By claiming that genetic mutations are a faster route to adaptation than RNA editing"},{"key":"D","text":"By suggesting that squid, not octopuses, rely most heavily on RNA editing"}]'::jsonb, 'B', null, 'Text 2 ''agrees'' editing works in the short term but stresses ''a hidden price'': fewer mutations over evolutionary time. Choice A is the trap for a reader expecting disagreement; the second team concedes the short-term benefit and disputes only the long-term bargain.') on conflict (id) do nothing;

