A transit planner argues that Route 5 has grown faster than Route 9 and should receive the next additional bus.', 'Which choice best describes data from the table that support the planner''s argument?', '[{"key":"A","text":"Route 9 gained more riders per day than Route 5 did between 2019 and 2024."},{"key":"B","text":"Both routes gained exactly 1,200 riders per day between 2019 and 2024."},{"key":"C","text":"Route 9 carried more riders than Route 5 in both years."},{"key":"D","text":"Route 5''s ridership rose by 30 percent, while Route 9''s rose by 10 percent."}]'::jsonb, 'D', null, 'Both routes gained 1,200 riders, but 1,200 is 30 percent of 4,000 and only 10 percent of 12,000, so Route 5 grew faster in the sense the planner means. Choice B is the trap: it is true, yet equal gains do not support a claim about faster growth.') on conflict (id) do nothing;
• The axolotl is a salamander native to lakes near Mexico City.
• It can regrow a lost limb, including bone, muscle, and nerves, within months.
• Unlike most salamanders, it keeps larval features such as external gills for its whole life.
• In the wild it is critically endangered because most of its lake habitat has been drained.
• Hundreds of thousands of axolotls live in laboratories and aquariums around the world.', 'The student wants to highlight a paradox in the axolotl''s situation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '[{"key":"A","text":"The axolotl, a salamander native to lakes near Mexico City, keeps its external gills for its whole life."},{"key":"B","text":"An axolotl can regrow a lost limb, including bone, muscle, and nerves, within months."},{"key":"C","text":"Although the axolotl is critically endangered in the wild, hundreds of thousands live in laboratories and aquariums around the world."},{"key":"D","text":"Most of the axolotl''s lake habitat near Mexico City has been drained."}]'::jsonb, 'C', null, 'A paradox needs two facts that seem to clash, and only choice C pairs them: nearly gone in the wild, yet numbering in the hundreds of thousands in captivity. The other choices each present one fact with nothing to contradict it.') on conflict (id) do nothing;
• Dorothea Lange photographed displaced farm families for a US government agency during the 1930s.
• Her 1936 photograph ''Migrant Mother'' became one of the most reproduced images of the Great Depression.
• The woman in the photograph, Florence Owens Thompson, was not publicly identified until 1978.
• Thompson later said the photograph had brought her no benefit.
• Lange''s field notes recorded the family''s circumstances but not Thompson''s name.', 'The student wants to emphasize the gap between the photograph''s fame and its subject''s obscurity. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '[{"key":"A","text":"Although ''Migrant Mother'' became one of the most reproduced images of the Depression, the woman it shows was not publicly identified until 1978 and said the photograph brought her nothing."},{"key":"B","text":"Dorothea Lange photographed displaced farm families for a government agency during the 1930s."},{"key":"C","text":"Lange''s field notes recorded the family''s circumstances."},{"key":"D","text":"''Migrant Mother'' was taken in 1936 by the photographer Dorothea Lange."}]'::jsonb, 'A', null, 'The goal sets fame against obscurity, and choice A holds both: ''most reproduced'' on one side, unnamed for four decades and unrewarded on the other. Choice D mentions the photograph''s origin but nothing about its subject.') on conflict (id) do nothing;
• A city is deciding whether to replace its sodium streetlights with LEDs.
• LEDs use about half as much electricity as sodium lamps.
• LEDs last roughly four times as long, which reduces maintenance.
• Early LED streetlights gave off a bluish light that residents in several cities found harsh.
• Warmer-toned LEDs are now available at a similar price.', 'The student wants to acknowledge a common objection to LED streetlights and explain why it may no longer apply. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '[{"key":"A","text":"LEDs use about half as much electricity as sodium lamps and last roughly four times as long."},{"key":"B","text":"A city is deciding whether to replace its sodium streetlights with LEDs."},{"key":"C","text":"Residents in several cities objected to the harsh bluish light of early LED streetlights."},{"key":"D","text":"Although early LED streetlights gave off a bluish light that many residents found harsh, warmer-toned LEDs are now available at a similar price."}]'::jsonb, 'D', null, 'The goal has two parts, state the objection and explain why it may be outdated, and choice D does both with ''Although... harsh'' and ''now available.'' Choice C is the trap: it states the objection and then stops.') on conflict (id) do nothing;

